Subject: p value in logistic regression
Message-ID: <H3yk6ckKfYs.VQVdUyaB9eIJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/H3yk6ckKfYs/m/VQVdUyaB9eIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,
In glm.set.v1, the value is calculated via :

deviance <- obj$null.deviance - obj$deviance

p.value <- 1-pchisq(deviance,df=1)

When I calculate the pvalue using the summary function for glm:

 summary.glm(obj)$coefficient["Treat","Pr(>|z|)"]

the result is different.  Is there a particular reason the pvalue is calculated using the deviance and chisq distribution and is one more appropriate than the other?

Thanks

Rose

From u38c56d0e82a34280 at groups.google.com  Wed May 20 02:29:04 2015
From: u38c56d0e82a34280 at groups.google.com (Dhruva Chandramohan)
Date: Wed, 20 May 2015 02:29:04 +0000
Subject: Re: [methylkit_discussion] p value in logistic regression
Message-ID: <H3yk6ckKfYs.DhuC2H5cTkUJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <H3yk6ckKfYs.VQVdUyaB9eIJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
References: <H3yk6ckKfYs.VQVdUyaB9eIJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/H3yk6ckKfYs/m/DhuC2H5cTkUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I didn't write the original code, but the deviance calculation is appropriate for comparing two models- in this case, the null model which just fits using the mean over all the samples  (single parameter) vs. a model where we include a treatment effect (two parameters). The Wikipedia article on deviance <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deviance_%28statistics%29> is short but mentions some of the relevant issues. (If we were just wanting to select a model, I believe we could just take the one with minimum AIC <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akaike_information_criterion>, but we need a p-value to do FDR since we have so many sites)

The top answer to this post <http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/108995/interpreting-residual-and-null-deviance-in-glm-r> deals with the specific issue you noticed- see if you're observing weird things in the degrees of freedom as the original post did (the number should be higher for the null then for the residual). From what I can tell, the basic issue is that R^2 does not cleanly generalize from the OLS to the GLM setting, with the result that one is forced to choose a pseudo-R squared which shares some but not all of the properties of the standard R^2. (see explanation here <http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/mult_pkg/faq/general/Psuedo_RSquareds.htm>). Hence the argument for using the deviance calculation.

