Actually, in re-reading your question, I think the second paragraph above is less relevant. The two procedures are measuring different thing- one directly the goodness of fit of the model, the other the likelihood of the parameter estimate. Note that using the deviance calculation makes sense in more general cases- such as adding another covariate. You can use a deviance calculation to compare ~Gender+Treatment vs. ~Treatment, but you can't simply compare the p-values of the Treatment coefficients.

